            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Middle Way</strong><br>\nAfter years of extreme asceticism, Siddhartha concluded that neither luxurious living nor extreme deprivation led to enlightenment. He adopted the Middle Way, a balanced approach to living. This path was neither too indulgent nor too austere and became a cornerstone of his future teachings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enlightenment under the Bodhi Tree</strong><br>\nSiddhartha sat under a Bodhi tree, vowing to meditate until he found the answers to suffering. After 49 days, he attained enlightenment and understood the nature of suffering, its causes, and the way to eliminate it. At this point, he became known as the Buddha, or \"the awakened one.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Buddha's First Teaching</strong><br>\nThe Buddha gave his first sermon in Deer Park, near Varanasi, where he shared the Four Noble Truths with his five former companions. These truths lay out the essence of human suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path leading to its end, known as the Noble Eightfold Path.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
